WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:05.230 --> 00:00:11.500
Let us move on now to item four on the agenda,
which is the NHS Performance Report. We will

00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:18.920
take this in three parts: Barbara to talk
to NHS performance, Paul to talk to Finance,

00:00:18.920 --> 00:00:23.119
and Karen to talk to NHS England performance.
Barbara, may we start with you.

00:00:23.119 --> 00:00:24.349
NHS Performance Report
Barbara Hakin

00:00:24.349 --> 00:00:25.820
National Director: Commissioning Operations,
NHS England

00:00:25.820 --> 00:00:28.269
Preamble
Thank you, Chairman. I think it is first and

00:00:28.269 --> 00:00:32.400
most important to say that the standard of
care and the performance against key indicators

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:37.170
and the quality of care that patients get
in England from our staff in the NHS is very,

00:00:37.170 --> 00:00:43.620
very high indeed. We have a range of things
that we in NHS England are specifically responsible

00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:49.460
for overseeing that are delivered, and I would
like to start with those Access Standards

00:00:49.460 --> 00:00:55.900
that fall within the NHS Constitution. There
are other things that are in the NHS Constitution,

00:00:55.900 --> 00:01:02.641
but the length of time that patients wait
to be seen or treated is absolutely a key

00:01:02.641 --> 00:01:03.987
part of the Constitution.
Access standards within the NHS Constitution

00:01:03.987 --> 00:01:07.040
Dip in standards
I do think it is really important to start

00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:12.290
with clear statements that the vast majority
of patients, whether they are in an A&amp;E department,

00:01:12.290 --> 00:01:16.070
whether they have been referred, or whether
they are waiting for an ambulance, are seen

00:01:16.070 --> 00:01:22.580
very swiftly and in a very timely fashion.
We do have a set of standards to ensure that

00:01:22.580 --> 00:01:29.670
we continue to give our patients the service
they want. Over the last few weeks we have

00:01:29.670 --> 00:01:35.131
seen a slight dip in the standards beyond
that that we would wish. Now, the standards

00:01:35.131 --> 00:01:39.050
are measured overall on period of time. You
can’t take a snapshot of the standards,

00:01:39.050 --> 00:01:44.430
for very good reasons of statistical significance
and because in some instances it is clinically

00:01:44.430 --> 00:01:47.820
appropriate for patients not to be seen or
treated quickly.

00:01:47.820 --> 00:01:53.260
It is clinically appropriate for some patients
to stay in the A&amp;E department for longer than

00:01:53.260 --> 00:01:59.860
four hours, however for the majority we do
want to ensure that they are seen much more

00:01:59.860 --> 00:02:04.750
quickly than that. In fact, if you look at
A&amp;E the vast majority of patients are seen

00:02:04.750 --> 00:02:09.979
and treated and either admitted or discharged
very quickly and way within the four-hour

00:02:09.979 --> 00:02:14.989
target. We have seen a dip in those standards
over the last few weeks, and so there is a

00:02:14.989 --> 00:02:19.540
great deal of work going on to ensure that
we can get those standards back up to where

00:02:19.540 --> 00:02:25.159
we want them to be.
You may well ask why there has been a dip

00:02:25.159 --> 00:02:31.959
in some of the standards. One thing for certain
is that this is not due to the NHS not working

00:02:31.959 --> 00:02:39.239
hard, not being effective. We have seen unprecedented
rises in demand and need; people are living

00:02:39.239 --> 00:02:43.189
longer, people are sicker, and the things
that we can do to help them have increased

00:02:43.189 --> 00:02:48.129
enormously. So we are seeing a continuing
rise in emergency admissions, a continuing

00:02:48.129 --> 00:02:53.999
rise in the number of patients who are referred
for planned treatment and, very specifically,

00:02:53.999 --> 00:03:00.579
a continuing rise in patients who are referred
for cancer. This is excellent, because we

00:03:00.579 --> 00:03:05.219
know that our cancer survival rates are not
as good as they should be, and so to see an

00:03:05.219 --> 00:03:09.780
increase in referrals when patients might
have cancer – so that this can be picked

00:03:09.780 --> 00:03:15.640
up early – is a key part of that. We now
need to redouble our efforts – and we have

00:03:15.640 --> 00:03:19.420
been doing over the last few weeks and months
– to make sure that the right capacity is

00:03:19.420 --> 00:03:25.609
in place and the right processes are in place
to deal with some of these increases in patient

00:03:25.609 --> 00:03:26.959
need.
Waiting time for planned care

00:03:26.959 --> 00:03:34.329
So if I start with the waiting time for patients
who have been referred for planned care, where

00:03:34.329 --> 00:03:40.099
we say that patients’ treatment should be
initiated – whether that is a course of

00:03:40.099 --> 00:03:47.989
tablets or whether it is an operation – within
18 weeks. Again, that target was not being

00:03:47.989 --> 00:03:52.610
met and it was apparent that there were some
patients on the waiting list who had been

00:03:52.610 --> 00:03:57.180
waiting longer than they should have done.
Therefore, given that care and safety for

00:03:57.180 --> 00:04:02.550
patients is a priority, we determined two
things: firstly that another £250 million

00:04:02.550 --> 00:04:06.640
will be put into the system in order to treat
those patients who are waiting longest, but

00:04:06.640 --> 00:04:11.359
secondly that there would be an amnesty on
the standard we were asking of Trusts, because

00:04:11.359 --> 00:04:18.060
plainly the 18-weeks Referral to Treatment
Standard is a ratio. So we try to make sure

00:04:18.060 --> 00:04:23.200
that 90% of patients are treated within 18
weeks, and if you are going to treat long-waiters

00:04:23.200 --> 00:04:28.770
then that ratio cannot be met. So over the
summer months and right through until November

00:04:28.770 --> 00:04:36.970
we have put additional sessions on in hospitals
in order to get back on track for the winter

00:04:36.970 --> 00:04:40.940
in terms of meeting that target.
Now, we have not done any of this in isolation.

00:04:40.940 --> 00:04:44.760
We are a commissioner of care and we have
a huge responsibility as the commissioner

00:04:44.760 --> 00:04:52.430
of care, and I cannot underestimate the importance
of our partner organisations in helping us,

00:04:52.430 --> 00:04:58.430
particularly the Trust Development Agency
and Monitor who oversee the provider organisations.

00:04:58.430 --> 00:05:04.150
One of the things we have done in an overarching
way for all of these standards is to work

00:05:04.150 --> 00:05:07.620
very much more closely together. Simon has
already talked about some other things he

00:05:07.620 --> 00:05:11.750
is doing with the other chief executives.
We have also put in place much more robust

00:05:11.750 --> 00:05:16.150
arrangements at regional level for our regional
directors to be working with their regional

00:05:16.150 --> 00:05:20.900
directors to help and support health economists
to ensure that these patients get the treatment

00:05:20.900 --> 00:05:26.200
in the time that they need.
We are also keeping a very close eye on diagnostic

00:05:26.200 --> 00:05:31.450
waiting times, because waiting for a diagnosis
is absolutely the worst thing for patients.

00:05:31.450 --> 00:05:37.141
We are also ensuring that we either purchase
extra sessions or we support the Trusts to

00:05:37.141 --> 00:05:43.010
be more efficient and effective in ensuring
these diagnostic tests are done.

00:05:43.010 --> 00:05:46.970
Similarly on cancer, not only are Tripartite
and Monitor working very closely with their

00:05:46.970 --> 00:05:50.910
trusts to get their cancer waiting times back
on track – they are just slightly below,

00:05:50.910 --> 00:05:55.210
as I said the vast majority of patients are
seen in an appropriate and timely fashion

00:05:55.210 --> 00:05:59.980
– we want to get that back on track. We
have put in place a Cancer Task Force which

00:05:59.980 --> 00:06:07.410
NHS England is supporting, but working with
our Tripartite colleagues to make sure that

00:06:07.410 --> 00:06:11.640
the best places are there to help those who
are struggling with this.

00:06:11.640 --> 00:06:15.030
A&amp;E four-hour standard
Also worrying of course is the A&amp;E four-hour

00:06:15.030 --> 00:06:20.120
standard, and that is a particular worry as
we are moving to the winter. Again, this is

00:06:20.120 --> 00:06:23.940
something where we have put in significant
additional resource and support across the

00:06:23.940 --> 00:06:29.840
Tripartite organisations. We are looking to
ensure that all our hospitals have the right

00:06:29.840 --> 00:06:34.970
capacity, whether that is in terms of beds
or the staff to deal with patients who need

00:06:34.970 --> 00:06:40.710
to be admitted, ensuring that out in the community
and working with our partners in social care

00:06:40.710 --> 00:06:44.370
everything is in place to ensure those people
who do not need to be admitted to hospital

00:06:44.370 --> 00:06:47.250
do not have to be.
We are also ensuring that A &amp; E departments,

00:06:47.250 --> 00:06:51.980
if they need to take on extra staff, have
the resource to do that. So a huge effort

00:06:51.980 --> 00:06:57.230
is going in to ensure that actually we are
prepared for the winter. Our A&amp;E departments

00:06:57.230 --> 00:07:05.020
I think are the barometer of the system. Often
we think that the problem is A &amp; E. The problem

00:07:05.020 --> 00:07:08.140
is not A &amp; E; the problem is that A&amp;E is the
barometer of what is going on in the rest

00:07:08.140 --> 00:07:11.010
of the system. So there is a huge amount of
effort to get that back on track.

00:07:11.010 --> 00:07:16.740
Similarly with ambulances; most of our ambulance
trusts are providing very good standards,

00:07:16.740 --> 00:07:21.390
but again we have seen some dip in performance
recently from some of them. So again, we are

00:07:21.390 --> 00:07:42.390
working with our Tripartite partners to support
that.

00:07:42.390 --> 00:07:50.090
Dementia
I thought it would be worthwhile having a

00:07:50.090 --> 00:07:54.210
word about dementia, given that it is topical
at the moment and given NHS England’s drive

00:07:54.210 --> 00:07:59.430
to make sure that patients need to be diagnosed
with dementia so that they can be properly

00:07:59.430 --> 00:08:03.010
supported. So I will hand over to Bruce on
that one, if I may.

00:08:03.010 --> 00:08:11.950
Bruce Keogh: There has been a debate about
the wisdom of trying to identify patients

00:08:11.950 --> 00:08:16.920
early with dementia, and I have to say, as
someone who has been part of a family where

00:08:16.920 --> 00:08:21.500
we have watched somebody go through to death
with dementia, it would have been enormously

00:08:21.500 --> 00:08:28.960
helpful to have known much earlier that the
dementia was beginning to emerge and to have

00:08:28.960 --> 00:08:33.930
had the kind of support that a family needs
under those kinds of circumstances to go through

00:08:33.930 --> 00:08:42.870
a very difficult period before the disease
really takes hold. So, early identification

00:08:42.870 --> 00:08:48.790
will in some cases enable some treatment which
will slow down the progression of the disease,

00:08:48.790 --> 00:08:53.990
and in the absence of that I think we have
a duty in our health service also to offer

00:08:53.990 --> 00:09:01.310
people the kind of help that particularly
families would like when they are about to

00:09:01.310 --> 00:09:11.199
see a member of the family deteriorate significantly.
Access to psychological therapies

00:09:11.199 --> 00:09:19.800
Barbara Hakin: I think it is just worth staying
on access to psychological therapies, which

00:09:19.800 --> 00:09:24.720
is something that is really important for
us in terms of Parity of Esteem. The numbers

00:09:24.720 --> 00:09:29.389
of patients who are being seen is rising very
significantly, and the number of patients

00:09:29.389 --> 00:09:34.810
who are benefitting significantly from treatment
is rising significantly. We have set ourselves

00:09:34.810 --> 00:09:41.959
an ambitious target for the numbers in both
those who have access to this treatment and

00:09:41.959 --> 00:09:49.709
benefit from it. We do need to continue with
the focus on ensuring that we increase the

00:09:49.709 --> 00:09:54.829
access rate in order to meet both of those
targets by the end of the year. There is a

00:09:54.829 --> 00:09:58.399
lot of work going on, and I can assure the
Board that we continue to be confident that

00:09:58.399 --> 00:10:06.980
we will achieve our ambition.
Malcolm Grant: On all of these, we as a Board

00:10:06.980 --> 00:10:12.829
are utterly committed to meeting the constitutional
pledges. It is part of our mandate, something

00:10:12.829 --> 00:10:18.970
we absolutely have to do. Yet we do not run
the hospitals, and the bulk of our commissioning

00:10:18.970 --> 00:10:26.370
purchases are CCGs, so Simon, what assurance
can you give us as to our ability over the

00:10:26.370 --> 00:10:34.129
winter to oversee and to develop the Tripartite
arrangements to ensure that winter does not

00:10:34.129 --> 00:10:39.430
cause a major falling away from the expectations
of the Constitution?

00:10:39.430 --> 00:10:45.110
Simon Stevens: There clearly are real service
pressures in the way that Barbara has described.

00:10:45.110 --> 00:10:48.620
The number of people presenting in A&amp;E departments
and the number of people having emergency

00:10:48.620 --> 00:10:54.329
admissions is continuing to go up. In some
sense, that is not unexpected. We have an

00:10:54.329 --> 00:11:00.160
ageing population, we have a growing population
and there are pressures in other parts of

00:11:00.160 --> 00:11:04.420
primary community social care services that
mean that some of the support that people

00:11:04.420 --> 00:11:07.980
would have previously received has been fraying
at the edges.

00:11:07.980 --> 00:11:15.279
Despite all of that, the reality is that across
the Health Service people are moving Heaven

00:11:15.279 --> 00:11:21.649
and Earth to ensure that services are in place
and are joined up, using some of the extra

00:11:21.649 --> 00:11:27.420
money that the Government has made available
to open extra community rehab beds, inpatient

00:11:27.420 --> 00:11:34.730
beds where that makes sense, and also within
hospitals addressing some of the well-known

00:11:34.730 --> 00:11:38.670
processes that are required to make sure that
people get admitted when they need to be and

00:11:38.670 --> 00:11:45.040
get discharged when they should.
So the reality is that, given the system we

00:11:45.040 --> 00:11:51.230
have, this is going to take a joint effort
between TDA, Monitor and NHS England at every

00:11:51.230 --> 00:11:58.110
level of the system, nationally, regionally,
locally and that is now in place. I think

00:11:58.110 --> 00:12:02.540
as seen from the perspective of CCGs, the
hospitals and local authorities, they would

00:12:02.540 --> 00:12:10.671
recognise that there is a more joined-up conversation
that is occurring between the three NHS national

00:12:10.671 --> 00:12:15.660
bodies and what they are doing locally.
Malcolm Grant: So we are seeing sufficiently

00:12:15.660 --> 00:12:18.500
strong local collaboration, coordination and
joint working?

00:12:18.500 --> 00:12:24.970
Simon Stevens: In most places, yes. Actually,
the requirements of the Better Care Fund have

00:12:24.970 --> 00:12:32.240
triggered some of those changes of behaviours.
Given the huge financial pressures in different

00:12:32.240 --> 00:12:35.689
parts of the system, including the local authorities,
there is no point pretending that there are

00:12:35.689 --> 00:12:43.709
not sometimes tensions, but for most of the
country we believe people are actually doing

00:12:43.709 --> 00:12:49.230
the right thing together. In a small number
of cases where there are still questions about

00:12:49.230 --> 00:12:55.579
that, then David Bennett, David Flory and
I are having joint face-to-face meetings with

00:12:55.579 --> 00:13:02.209
the leaders of those local organisations to
make sure that nobody can interpret signals

00:13:02.209 --> 00:13:08.120
they are getting from the different NHS organisations
as supportive of behaviours that are anything

00:13:08.120 --> 00:13:12.249
other than joined up.
Malcolm Grant: That is reassuring.

00:13:12.249 --> 00:13:15.399
Q&amp;A
At this stage can I take any questions or

00:13:15.399 --> 00:13:19.980
comments on the Report so far?
David Roberts: Simon and Barbara, is this

00:13:19.980 --> 00:13:26.819
a uniform problem in targets across the country
or is it concentrated in certain areas, and

00:13:26.819 --> 00:13:33.869
if it is the latter, are we providing intensive
support through the Tripartite to help those

00:13:33.869 --> 00:13:39.730
people improve their performance?
Simon Stevens: Yes, the activity increases

00:13:39.730 --> 00:13:46.819
are occurring on a fairly broad basis across
the country, but the reality is that some

00:13:46.819 --> 00:13:51.439
parts of the country are definitely more pressurised
than others. Individual institutions are more

00:13:51.439 --> 00:13:55.190
pressurised than others. In some cases it
might be because they have a physical capacity

00:13:55.190 --> 00:14:04.389
constraint. So we are taking a pretty differentiated
approach: those places that are doing well

00:14:04.389 --> 00:14:10.910
continue to do so; those places that are struggling
are getting more intensive support, shall

00:14:10.910 --> 00:14:17.889
we say, including best practice through best
practice teams, ECIST. We have collectively

00:14:17.889 --> 00:14:25.060
asked providers and CCGs what they think would
make the most difference with the extra funding

00:14:25.060 --> 00:14:29.709
that the government has made available for
winter. I am understanding that will mostly

00:14:29.709 --> 00:14:36.819
come online probably during the back end of
November/December, so that will provide some

00:14:36.819 --> 00:14:42.490
extra lift in performance, but it is going
to be tight.

00:14:42.490 --> 00:14:53.059
Ciarán Devane: Back to dementia, having spoken
to my colleagues who work in this area, particularly

00:14:53.059 --> 00:14:57.629
in the voluntary sector, they are very supportive
of the move because of the idea that just

00:14:57.629 --> 00:15:02.209
because there is not a tablet or a medical
intervention does not mean that there is not

00:15:02.209 --> 00:15:07.329
an intervention. I think that whole movement
to identify people so that the right care

00:15:07.329 --> 00:15:12.679
plan can be wrapped around them or at least
offered is hugely important. So, just to say

00:15:12.679 --> 00:15:14.869
that I think it is clearly the right thing
to be doing.

00:15:14.869 --> 00:15:19.790
Barbara Hakin: We do have a significant number
of things next year. We have made dementia

00:15:19.790 --> 00:15:24.800
a priority as to the things we do for patients
with dementia next year in the contract, as

00:15:24.800 --> 00:15:30.759
part of this drive to make sure that all the
patients who have dementia are properly identified,

00:15:30.759 --> 00:15:36.480
because we do see a very, very significant
variation which would lead you to believe

00:15:36.480 --> 00:15:41.739
that in some places, very high numbers of
patients with dementia are not being identified.

00:15:41.739 --> 00:15:46.279
If they are not identified, then the summary
care records do not identify that. If that

00:15:46.279 --> 00:15:52.459
patient goes to A&amp;E they do not get the right
package of care, so it is really important

00:15:52.459 --> 00:15:59.529
that we reduce those inequalities.
Malcolm Grant: Going back to the constitution

00:15:59.529 --> 00:16:04.179
standards, I know we will be looking at this
at every meeting forthcoming of the Board

00:16:04.179 --> 00:16:10.029
and any early notification informally to Board
meetings, if there is a blip in performance,

00:16:10.029 --> 00:16:12.209
would be appreciated.
Finance Report

00:16:12.209 --> 00:16:15.249
Paul Baumann
Chief Financial Officer, NHS England

00:16:15.249 --> 00:16:20.989
Preamble
You will recall the last time we met in the

00:16:20.989 --> 00:16:26.070
beginning of September that I talked about
the stock-take that we were due to complete

00:16:26.070 --> 00:16:32.270
around the month six, the cumulative September
activities and financial consequences. That

00:16:32.270 --> 00:16:35.350
stock-take has now been largely completed
and I will come to that largely at the end

00:16:35.350 --> 00:16:41.910
of this. The result of that is what you can
see under section 15, which is the Finance

00:16:41.910 --> 00:16:46.259
report, or for those of you who like me prefer
to look at surpluses rather than expenditure

00:16:46.259 --> 00:16:52.630
because it is slightly more encouraging, annexe
C table 1 has the financial detail in it.

00:16:52.630 --> 00:16:53.649
Financial report
Risk

00:16:53.649 --> 00:16:57.209
You will recall that when I talked to you
about this in September, we had a forecast

00:16:57.209 --> 00:17:02.610
which at that stage was pretty well bang-on
the plan for the year, but I made comments

00:17:02.610 --> 00:17:07.600
about the extent to which that was to be enjoyed
with some degree of caution. Indeed, if you

00:17:07.600 --> 00:17:12.159
looked then last time at the risk and opportunity
assessment that we made, there was quite a

00:17:12.159 --> 00:17:18.409
wide range of about £366 million of risks
that I talked through on that occasion, which

00:17:18.409 --> 00:17:23.720
would have resulted in an overspend of that
magnitude had those risks come about.

00:17:23.720 --> 00:17:28.140
As you would expect as we go through the year,
the range of outcomes narrows as we get better

00:17:28.140 --> 00:17:32.920
sight on things, and as you can see from the
table in front of you – either of the tables

00:17:32.920 --> 00:17:39.460
I talked about – approximately half of that
£360 million-odd of risk has manifested itself

00:17:39.460 --> 00:17:44.299
in the forecast as we have now presented it,
with a potential overspend in the forecast

00:17:44.299 --> 00:17:49.050
of £184 million of which, as you can see,
only £23 million has come through in the

00:17:49.050 --> 00:17:55.510
month six year-to-date results. The forecast
going forward suggests that that will worsen

00:17:55.510 --> 00:18:00.880
to £184 million. It is always worth remembering
that millions numbers in the NHS sound very

00:18:00.880 --> 00:18:07.190
big because we are a big organisation; £184
million is, as you can see there, a 0.2% variation

00:18:07.190 --> 00:18:12.600
on the resources that we are managing in the
system, so to be taken seriously but not a

00:18:12.600 --> 00:18:16.390
vast deviation, shall we say.
A patchy picture

00:18:16.390 --> 00:18:21.299
As always, a bit of a patchy picture, you
can see there. CCGs as a whole are £21 million

00:18:21.299 --> 00:18:26.929
over drift, more or less point landing on
the plan at this stage. The number of deficits

00:18:26.929 --> 00:18:33.820
has in fact fallen back from 20 we had at
month four. The paper says 19; I was being

00:18:33.820 --> 00:18:38.429
slightly ungenerous with my rounding of one
of those. It is in practice 18 deficits that

00:18:38.429 --> 00:18:42.529
we have, which is exactly in line with the
number of deficits we had at the beginning

00:18:42.529 --> 00:18:46.260
of the year.
We talked a bit last time about recovery plans

00:18:46.260 --> 00:18:51.120
and the way we were supporting CCGs to implement
their recovery plans. I guess I have good

00:18:51.120 --> 00:18:55.289
news in the sense that three of them have
recovery plans which, as reflected in this

00:18:55.289 --> 00:19:00.240
forecast, has taken two of them back out of
deficit, one of them to a reduced deficit.

00:19:00.240 --> 00:19:04.370
As always in the NHS, there are a few going
the other way; there are three who have become

00:19:04.370 --> 00:19:11.420
more negative in a specific way in this forecast.
On balance, CCGs are pretty well holding the

00:19:11.420 --> 00:19:17.399
picture there. You can see perhaps that there
is something to be done in particular in the

00:19:17.399 --> 00:19:21.380
Midlands and East region, which has a bit
of recovery to do between their year-to-date

00:19:21.380 --> 00:19:27.250
performance and the end of the year, which
is not without risk. But the overall conclusion

00:19:27.250 --> 00:19:32.490
we drew from the stock-take was that there
was if anything a modest upside in the CCG

00:19:32.490 --> 00:19:39.270
financial position taken across the country
rather than a substantial risk of it deteriorating.

00:19:39.270 --> 00:19:42.000
Specialised commissioning
Moving further down the picture, the position

00:19:42.000 --> 00:19:48.919
on specialised commissioning is forecast overspend
of £167 million, or 1.2%. The biggest element

00:19:48.919 --> 00:19:53.190
we are going to be talking about later on
in the agenda today, which is the Cancer Drugs

00:19:53.190 --> 00:19:58.330
Fund. Taking the core activity of specialised
commissioning after all the mitigations and

00:19:58.330 --> 00:20:04.519
QIPP things that are being done, we are expecting
to be less than 1% overspent by the end of

00:20:04.519 --> 00:20:08.250
the year in the forecast. That is still an
overspend, but it is an improving overspend,

00:20:08.250 --> 00:20:14.091
if you like, in the year-on-year trend. Primary
care is looking slightly favourable to plan

00:20:14.091 --> 00:20:19.440
across the country, which once again partially
offsets the specialised overspend that we

00:20:19.440 --> 00:20:20.440
have.
Legacy

00:20:20.440 --> 00:20:27.200
You will see a little odd item towards the
bottom of annexe C, table 1, which is the

00:20:27.200 --> 00:20:32.409
£40 million-odd overspend on legacy expenditure.
You will remember that we spent a lot of time

00:20:32.409 --> 00:20:36.649
last year talking about legacy and its impact.
I have taken a fairly prudent view of some

00:20:36.649 --> 00:20:41.289
of things that are coming through in ’14/’15
still relating to the transition from PCTs.

00:20:41.289 --> 00:20:45.649
I am very much hoping that I will be able
to report a slightly better position on that

00:20:45.649 --> 00:20:47.491
once we have worked our way through all of
it.

00:20:47.491 --> 00:20:50.340
Concluding remarks
That is it. That leaves us £184 million off-plan

00:20:50.340 --> 00:20:54.649
in the forecast. I am pleased to say, though,
that the gap after risks and mitigations,

00:20:54.649 --> 00:21:00.720
which I have commented on in the report, reduces
from £184 million to £122 million. That

00:21:00.720 --> 00:21:04.280
is good news. The Board will, however, perhaps
want to know what on Earth we are doing about

00:21:04.280 --> 00:21:08.529
the £122 million that that leaves, and indeed,
the fact that there will be undoubtedly as

00:21:08.529 --> 00:21:12.600
the year progresses other things that we are
not aware of which emerge.

00:21:12.600 --> 00:21:17.649
I have already mentioned a couple of potential
positive movements in the CCG position and

00:21:17.649 --> 00:21:21.860
the legacy cost. One area, as I mentioned,
in which we have not finished the stock-take

00:21:21.860 --> 00:21:27.399
yet is central spending on running costs,
and I have the delight next week of spending

00:21:27.399 --> 00:21:32.080
more or less the whole week with colleagues
around this table discussing in-year spending

00:21:32.080 --> 00:21:36.080
on running costs and programme costs. I do
anticipate, just looking at the year-to-date

00:21:36.080 --> 00:21:39.679
trends and some of the actions that I think
we are going to be taking, that we will be

00:21:39.679 --> 00:21:44.539
able to recognise some positive reduction
in central costs as a consequence of that,

00:21:44.539 --> 00:21:50.840
which will start to offset the £122 million.
Taking together a number of smaller areas

00:21:50.840 --> 00:21:56.870
of focus, that gives us I think overall a
good chance of squaring the position in most

00:21:56.870 --> 00:22:01.100
foreseeable scenarios through to the end of
the year. £122 million, as I say, is a significant

00:22:01.100 --> 00:22:05.250
amount of money, but it is in percentage terms
a fairly small correction.

00:22:05.250 --> 00:22:08.360
The thing I would have to say to the Board
though is that there is very little, indeed

00:22:08.360 --> 00:22:15.500
no reserve left to cater for things that we
have not anticipated in the risk and mitigation

00:22:15.500 --> 00:22:19.230
analysis that we have done. This feels, as
I have said all through the year, to be the

00:22:19.230 --> 00:22:24.210
year in which we are absolutely on the knife-edge
of balancing or not balancing against the

00:22:24.210 --> 00:22:28.899
position that we have. As I have pointed out
before, the position we have is a reduction

00:22:28.899 --> 00:22:33.240
in cumulative surplus to the tune of about
£400 million, so it is really, really important

00:22:33.240 --> 00:22:39.480
that we contain our expenditure within that.
At the moment I can see ways in which it should

00:22:39.480 --> 00:22:44.019
be possible for us to do that, but as I say,
with absolutely nothing whatsoever to spare

00:22:44.019 --> 00:22:48.919
around that.
Malcolm Grant: You used the language, ‘A

00:22:48.919 --> 00:22:56.490
good chance of squaring the position by year-end.’
What assurance can you give us that we will

00:22:56.490 --> 00:23:01.269
remain within our cash limits?
Paul Baumann: These things are always subject

00:23:01.269 --> 00:23:04.720
to variation, but I suppose what I am trying
to say is that the net assessment from the

00:23:04.720 --> 00:23:09.799
system coming forward is £122 million of
overspend against the targets that we have

00:23:09.799 --> 00:23:15.950
set. I can identify as I look at the position
at least £122 million of positive things

00:23:15.950 --> 00:23:21.679
that I can build in and would expect to build
into month seven reporting. What I cannot

00:23:21.679 --> 00:23:25.659
guarantee to the Board is that there will
be nothing that transpires between now and

00:23:25.659 --> 00:23:30.419
the end of the year that is unexpected, either
in terms of activity trends going faster than

00:23:30.419 --> 00:23:36.919
we have in forecast, or indeed other calamities
or disasters that might befall us. In other

00:23:36.919 --> 00:23:39.630
years, it might have been possible to have
said, ‘But do not worry about that too much

00:23:39.630 --> 00:23:45.270
because I have a reserve over here of a couple
of hundred million that will deal with the

00:23:45.270 --> 00:23:51.830
inevitable bumps and turns that the NHS throws
up in the course of any given year. I have

00:23:51.830 --> 00:23:56.200
to tell you now that I do not have such a
£200 million, £100 million or indeed £50

00:23:56.200 --> 00:24:01.700
million pot sitting somewhere waiting to soak
up those bumps, so with that rider I have

00:24:01.700 --> 00:24:05.281
the means of squaring the position, which
is why I chose my words carefully. But I do

00:24:05.281 --> 00:24:08.789
not have the guarantee that we can deal with
all comers, as it were.

00:24:08.789 --> 00:24:12.179
Malcolm Grant: I understand. I invite the
Chair of the Audit Committee, Ed, to comment.

00:24:12.179 --> 00:24:18.799
Ed Smith: I think Paul has put it well. It
is not a place you particularly want to be

00:24:18.799 --> 00:24:25.720
in, because in this system we are very dependent
on a lot of moving parts coming in exactly

00:24:25.720 --> 00:24:31.679
where they now say they are going to come
in, and if they do not, and if external circumstances

00:24:31.679 --> 00:24:39.409
in CCGs, in specialised commissions, in activity
impact, then we do not have – and we drilled

00:24:39.409 --> 00:24:45.160
down in this on Monday at the Finance and
Investment Committee in some detail – the

00:24:45.160 --> 00:24:50.880
type of reserves, contingencies, buffers and
offsets that would allow us to come back.

00:24:50.880 --> 00:24:57.610
We had those last year. We needed them. We
have a degree of drawdown of previous surplus,

00:24:57.610 --> 00:25:04.870
but we cannot use more than a certain amount.
This is extraordinarily tight this year, before

00:25:04.870 --> 00:25:12.140
you even start on next year. The balance of
risk is that with a great finance director,

00:25:12.140 --> 00:25:18.159
he will balance the books, but I am not sure
it is a place I would feel terribly comfortable

00:25:18.159 --> 00:25:26.549
being in on a recurring basis.
Malcolm Grant: Thank you Paul for that report.

00:25:26.549 --> 00:25:27.679
NHS England Performance
Karen Wheeler

00:25:27.679 --> 00:25:29.730
National Director for Transformation and Corporate
Operations, NHS England

00:25:29.730 --> 00:25:36.240
Preamble
I just wanted to report on three aspects of

00:25:36.240 --> 00:25:42.779
NHS England’s specific performance versus
NHS performance, which Barbara has covered.

00:25:42.779 --> 00:25:47.500
Those are the business plan, major programmes
and overall corporate risks.

00:25:47.500 --> 00:25:49.159
NHS England performance
Business plan

00:25:49.159 --> 00:25:54.769
The business plan, as you might imagine, covers
pretty much comprehensively what we envisaged

00:25:54.769 --> 00:26:00.279
at the start of the year was in our plan.
Obviously, things have changed during the

00:26:00.279 --> 00:26:06.780
course of the year, but this report provides
you with assurance that the vast majority

00:26:06.780 --> 00:26:13.590
of those are on-track, and those which are
reporting as amber-red have largely been covered

00:26:13.590 --> 00:26:25.059
because they are as a result of issues that
Barbara has covered in NHS performance, or

00:26:25.059 --> 00:26:32.320
are coming up with transforming care as well
which I know Jane is going to cover later,

00:26:32.320 --> 00:26:38.659
and specialised services commissioning which
relates to the budget issues that Paul has

00:26:38.659 --> 00:26:44.389
covered off as well.
Customer contact reporting is amber. It is

00:26:44.389 --> 00:26:50.330
a basket of measures there which I reported
last time; I think we are just summarising

00:26:50.330 --> 00:26:57.409
those in an amber rating, but as we implement
a new customer management system by the end

00:26:57.409 --> 00:27:03.270
of the year we expect to see some significant
improvement on that performance.

00:27:03.270 --> 00:27:08.539
Major programmes
On major programmes, again we track and manage

00:27:08.539 --> 00:27:16.139
our major programmes across the organisation.
I have just picked out those which are reporting

00:27:16.139 --> 00:27:25.740
red or amber-red, and again what you see from
the report are two areas that we are separately

00:27:25.740 --> 00:27:34.070
covering: Transforming Care, and Parity of
Esteem has been referred to already in respect

00:27:34.070 --> 00:27:38.990
of the dementia issue.
Child protection information sharing I can

00:27:38.990 --> 00:27:49.070
actually report since this report was done
has now moved to amber, because work is on-track

00:27:49.070 --> 00:27:53.389
with the implementation of first-wave sites.
That is an improving position. Care data and

00:27:53.389 --> 00:28:02.840
data services for commissioners, are both
being managed quite assiduously through quite

00:28:02.840 --> 00:28:07.389
strong governance, but there are significant
risks associated with both of those.

00:28:07.389 --> 00:28:10.789
Corporate risks
Then the summary of corporate risks, I was

00:28:10.789 --> 00:28:14.120
not proposing to go through all of those.
As you might imagine, obviously there are

00:28:14.120 --> 00:28:21.580
some red risks there, and I am not sure there
is anything on that list that we have not

00:28:21.580 --> 00:28:26.690
either already covered or that you would not
expect to be there. This is really just to

00:28:26.690 --> 00:28:29.860
assure you that the delivery across NHS England
is being managed quite tightly.

00:28:29.860 --> 00:28:38.539
Ed Smith: I would say that the Audit Committee
looks at this with quite a degree of intensity,

00:28:38.539 --> 00:28:52.889
and I have three Audit Committee colleagues
around the table. In particular, we are taking

00:28:52.889 --> 00:28:58.470
the corporate risk section and doing deep
dives with the teams to really understand

00:28:58.470 --> 00:29:03.559
this. We did organisational alignment last
time. We will have a schedule of doing this.

00:29:03.559 --> 00:29:08.159
It is really important that we look at the
assurance mechanisms that move from gross

00:29:08.159 --> 00:29:14.950
to net risk on this schedule, but that the
Board keeps oversight of the overall risk

00:29:14.950 --> 00:29:20.649
agenda.

